.claude/settings.local.json

# docs/ IS DEFAULT-DENY (CCP-1214). Everything under docs/ is maintainer
# working state unless scripts/lib/docs-framework-allowlist.txt says it is
# framework content. The `!` lines below are that file's entries, one for one
# -- see "the two registers" at the end of this block.
#
# WHY THE RULE IS INVERTED. This used to ENUMERATE the working state:
# docs/HANDOVER.md, docs/.handover-archive/, docs/decisions/, docs/workitems/,
# docs/memory/, docs/workitems-idmap.yml, and a docs/.* dotfile pattern. An
# enumeration only covers what its author thought of, and measured on
# 16.09.2026 it had fallen 24 top-level entries (145 paths) behind, 21 of
# them genuine working state: every Full-Track phase folder (discovery/,
# concept/, validation/, architecture/, planning/, quality/, launch/,
# operations/, reviews/), the Lean-Track and session artefacts (instincts.md,
# BASELINE.md, FRAME.md, CLAUDE-lean.md, LEARNINGS.md, PROMOTION_BRIEF.md,
# TRACK_DECISION.md) and five further folders (concepts/, epics/, roadmaps/,
# user-stories/, lean-archive/). None of them was ignored, and none was
# allowlisted either. (The remaining 3 are the sweep erring wide on purpose:
# README.md and somewhere/ occur only as illustrations in comments, and chore
# is a false positive off "feat/fix/refactor/docs/chore". Over-inclusion can
# only cause a spurious failure somebody reads; under-inclusion is a path the
# guard stops seeing. CCP-1214 itself reported 16, sweeping
# commands/ and scripts/ for the phase folders and sprint artefacts and
# counting BACKLOG.md / SPRINT.md / PROJECT_PLAN.md at the top level where the
# shipped commands spell them under planning/; the figure here is the same
# method widened to hooks/, agents/ and templates/ and to every docs/ path
# those files name, which is what test_docs_boundary_default_deny.py
# asserts.) commands/postmortem.md
# tells a maintainer to store project-specific instincts in docs/instincts.md,
# so the command that ENDS a CCPR session wrote an untracked, un-ignored file
# into CCPR's own repository -- one `git add -A` away from the accident
# CCP-1017 records as having already happened once, when 17 CCPR work items
# were committed and pushed into what every user reads as their own state.
#
# CCP-1057 already chose a PATTERN over an enumeration here, deliberately and
# correctly: "a pattern nobody has to remember to extend is what actually
# keeps pace with a script adding a new report next quarter". The pattern it
# picked was docs/.*, which matches dotfiles only, while every artefact above
# is a normal filename. This is that same argument at its full width, not a
# reversal of it -- and docs/* subsumes docs/.*, since a gitignore `*` matches
# a leading dot (pinned by test_docs_boundary_default_deny.py).
#
# WHAT THE ENUMERATED LINES USED TO SAY, kept because the rules they justified
# are now implicit in docs/*:
#   * HANDOVER.md, .handover-archive/ and decisions/ are session artefacts,
#     not distribution. Keeping them untracked also keeps tenant identifiers
#     out of shipped artefacts (Constitution, Inviolable "No personal or
#     tenant data"). Decision drafts are extracted into a de-personalised ADR
#     under docs/adr/, which is why decisions/ needs no tracked home.
#   * workitems/ and memory/ are 89 work items about CCPR's own defects
#     (560 KB) and 400 KB of persona review notes, which would arrive in an
#     installation looking like the user's own state.
#   * workitems-idmap.yml is the id map `migrate` writes -- a snapshot of one
#     machine's migration. Its old comment argued it needed a line of its own
#     because it sat outside docs/workitems/ AND outside the docs/.* dotfile
#     pattern, "so neither of them covers it". That was true of the two
#     patterns it named and is no longer true of docs/*, which covers it like
#     any other depth-1 entry. The line is gone; the reasoning is why the rule
#     above has to be wide.
#
# THE FLAT TIER-1 MEMORY FILES, corrected 23.08.2026 after measuring rather
# than reading. This paragraph once ended "..., the flat Tier-1 memory files)
# stay tracked". That was never true: `git ls-files docs/memory` has always
# returned nothing. Tracking those 8 files (80 KB) later is still NOT a
# one-line change, and default-deny does not make it one. The allowlist serves
# BOTH sides of the boundary, so "tracked but not shipped" remains
# inexpressible: artifact-gate.sh fails a tracked docs/ path that is not
# listed, and install.sh copies exactly what IS listed. Measured 23.08.2026 --
# all 8 files fail the gate's docs-boundary check. Making them tracked-but-
# local means either moving them to a framework home that earns an allowlist
# entry, or splitting the allowlist into two lists, which is an ADR-sized
# change.
#
# THE TWO MECHANISMS STILL COVER DIFFERENT SETS. Since WI-0018, install.sh no
# longer copies docs/ wholesale: install_docs() filters each top-level docs/
# child against scripts/lib/docs-framework-allowlist.txt and reports what it
# skipped. This ignore rule is therefore not the only thing keeping working
# state out of an installation -- but it stays the first line, and it is the
# one that also keeps the state out of a clone. The allowlist is per top-level
# entry and reads the FILESYSTEM, not the git index, so an ignored file inside
# an ALLOWLISTED directory is still installed (see docs/logo/ below). Inverting
# this rule does not close that gap and was not meant to: the `!` lines
# re-open the allowlisted directories, so e.g. docs/adr/.session-context.md is
# not ignored here either. (docs/adr/.DS_Store IS ignored, but by the
# **/.DS_Store rule at the top of this file, not by anything in this block.)
# That hole is CCP-1156's and it stays open. It is CHARACTERISED -- stated in
# executable form, not fixed -- by test_docs_boundary_default_deny.py's
# ReincludedDirectoriesAreTheKnownBlindSpotTest. It is deliberately NOT in
# test_docs_dotfile_gitignore_coverage.py, whose sweep anchors on `docs/\.`
# and structurally cannot produce a path inside a subdirectory; that was
# tried, and a mutation adding docs/adr/.session-context.md to a shipped
# command left its suite green.
#
# THE TWO REGISTERS, AND WHY A TEST HOLDS THEM TOGETHER. The `!` lines are a
# THIRD copy of the allowlist, beside artifact-gate.sh's reader and
# install.sh's. CCP-1017's PO decision accepted that cost in advance and named
# the condition: "two lists that must agree are exactly the shape that
# produced WI-0059 ... derive one from the other or pin their agreement with a
# test". A .gitignore cannot include another file, so derivation is
# unavailable and the pin is the obligation --
# scripts/tests/test_docs_boundary_default_deny.py reads both files
# independently and fails whichever one is edited alone.
#
# ADDING A FRAMEWORK DOCUMENT IS THEREFORE TWO LINES, not one: the allowlist
# entry (so the gate accepts it and install.sh ships it) and the `!docs/` line
# here (so this repository still tracks it). Forgetting either is a test
# failure, never a silent miss.
#
# ORDER AND FORM ARE LOAD-BEARING, measured in a throwaway repo rather than
# assumed. `docs/*` + `!docs/adr/` works; `docs/` + `!docs/adr/` erases the
# whole tree, because git never descends into an excluded DIRECTORY and the
# re-inclusion can no longer be reached. A `!` line placed BEFORE docs/* is
# overruled by it -- last matching pattern wins. Both are pinned.
#
# A project generated by scripts/project-init.sh still gets an ENUMERATED
# dotfile block instead of this rule, and that difference is deliberate: a
# user's docs/ is their own working directory and may legitimately track
# things CCPR's docs/ never does. Default-deny is right for a repository whose
# docs/ ships as framework content; it would be wrong for theirs.
docs/*
!docs/adr/
!docs/logo/
!docs/CONSTITUTION.md
!docs/NEXT_STEPS_REFERENCE.md
!docs/PROJECT_PHASES.md

# The project's own Claude settings. `migrate` creates or rewrites this file to
# flip `workitems.provider`, and a youtrack-backed project must put its
# `baseUrl` and token path in it first -- a real infrastructure address and
# machine-specific paths, in a repository meant to be cloned. The setup those
# values belong to is documented separately; the values themselves are per
# installation. `.claude/settings.local.json` was already ignored above; this
# closes the other half.
.claude/settings.json

# Social-preview renders. The tracked logo assets under docs/logo/ (icon SVGs,
# wordmark PNGs) are referenced from the README; these two are not referenced
# from anywhere in the repo -- they exist for a platform's repo-preview slot,
# which is configured in the host's settings rather than from a file.
# They live under assets/, NOT docs/logo/, and that placement is the point:
# docs/logo/ is allowlisted as a whole in scripts/lib/docs-framework-allowlist.txt
# and install_docs() reads the FILESYSTEM rather than the git index, so an ignored
# file in an allowlisted directory is still copied into every installation --
# measured on 21.08.2026, when deleting them from ~/.claude/docs/logo/ was undone
# by the very next `install.sh --update`. assets/ is in no artifact list, so
# nothing here ships. Ignored on top of that, to keep `git status` quiet.
assets/

